1
00:00:00,116 --> 00:00:03,320
Hello and welcome back to J D Science.

2
00:00:04,029 --> 00:00:07,639
In today's T-Level Chemistry lesson, we are going to study A

3
00:00:07,721 --> 00:00:10,124
twelve point six, The Haber Process.

4
00:00:10,646 --> 00:00:13,572
By the end of this lesson, you should be able to state

5
00:00:13,618 --> 00:00:17,322
typical Haber conditions, explain their economic effect,

6
00:00:17,368 --> 00:00:21,165
and describe the compromise between equilibrium yield and reaction

7
00:00:21,234 --> 00:00:21,629
rate.

8
00:00:22,152 --> 00:00:25,913
The equation is nitrogen plus three hydrogen in equilibrium

9
00:00:26,029 --> 00:00:27,167
with two ammonia.

10
00:00:27,620 --> 00:00:30,917
The forward reaction has delta H less than zero.

11
00:00:31,312 --> 00:00:32,600
It is exothermic.

12
00:00:32,937 --> 00:00:36,571
The industrial aim is ammonia at a useful rate and

13
00:00:36,629 --> 00:00:40,263
a useful yield, at an economically acceptable cost.

14
00:00:40,600 --> 00:00:44,535
Typical conditions in the lesson are about four hundred and fifty degrees

15
00:00:44,593 --> 00:00:48,204
Celsius, about two hundred atmospheres, and an iron

16
00:00:48,274 --> 00:00:49,028
catalyst.

17
00:00:49,423 --> 00:00:53,568
Unreacted nitrogen and hydrogen can be separated from ammonia

18
00:00:53,626 --> 00:00:54,624
and recycled.

19
00:00:55,077 --> 00:00:58,607
Conditions are not chosen to maximise one factor alone.

20
00:00:59,059 --> 00:01:00,545
Retrieve Le Chatelier.

21
00:01:00,882 --> 00:01:04,063
Lower temperature favours the exothermic direction.

22
00:01:04,400 --> 00:01:07,383
Increasing pressure favours fewer gas moles.

23
00:01:07,592 --> 00:01:11,377
A catalyst lowers activation energy and increases rate,

24
00:01:11,551 --> 00:01:13,931
but does not change the equilibrium position.

25
00:01:14,059 --> 00:01:15,266
Count the gas moles.

26
00:01:15,603 --> 00:01:19,144
One nitrogen plus three hydrogen is four moles of gas.

27
00:01:19,423 --> 00:01:21,792
Two ammonia is two moles of gas.

28
00:01:22,001 --> 00:01:25,844
Increasing pressure favours the side with fewer gas moles, so

29
00:01:25,902 --> 00:01:29,663
the equilibrium shifts toward ammonia and the equilibrium yield

30
00:01:29,745 --> 00:01:31,254
of ammonia increases.

31
00:01:31,591 --> 00:01:33,251
Temperature is a compromise.

32
00:01:33,587 --> 00:01:37,383
Lower temperature gives a higher ammonia equilibrium yield,

33
00:01:37,534 --> 00:01:41,180
because it favours the exothermic forward reaction, but the

34
00:01:41,261 --> 00:01:42,329
rate is slower.

35
00:01:42,782 --> 00:01:46,799
Higher temperature gives a faster rate, but a lower equilibrium

36
00:01:46,880 --> 00:01:50,201
yield, because it favours the endothermic reverse.

37
00:01:50,654 --> 00:01:54,532
About four hundred and fifty degrees Celsius is fast enough,

38
00:01:54,613 --> 00:01:56,842
with a useful equilibrium yield.

39
00:01:57,121 --> 00:02:00,035
That is a compromise between rate and yield.

40
00:02:00,162 --> 00:02:02,206
Pressure is also a compromise.

41
00:02:02,484 --> 00:02:06,211
Higher pressure increases ammonia equilibrium yield.

42
00:02:06,548 --> 00:02:10,344
Extremely high pressure needs more energy for compression,

43
00:02:10,553 --> 00:02:14,257
stronger and more expensive equipment, and higher capital

44
00:02:14,338 --> 00:02:15,720
and operating costs.

45
00:02:15,964 --> 00:02:19,574
About two hundred atmospheres is high, but economically

46
00:02:19,632 --> 00:02:20,317
practical.

47
00:02:20,596 --> 00:02:23,255
Exact plant conditions can vary.

48
00:02:23,498 --> 00:02:27,353
The specification focus is the compromise, not one

49
00:02:27,399 --> 00:02:28,665
global optimum.

50
00:02:29,060 --> 00:02:32,694
The iron catalyst provides an alternative pathway with

51
00:02:32,786 --> 00:02:34,447
lower activation energy.

52
00:02:34,841 --> 00:02:38,707
It increases the rate of the forward and reverse reactions,

53
00:02:38,858 --> 00:02:41,261
so equilibrium is reached faster.

54
00:02:41,598 --> 00:02:45,290
It does not change the equilibrium position, the equilibrium

55
00:02:45,371 --> 00:02:49,493
composition, or the equilibrium yield at fixed temperature

56
00:02:49,551 --> 00:02:50,329
and pressure.

57
00:02:50,782 --> 00:02:52,836
It does not change delta H.

58
00:02:53,173 --> 00:02:56,749
It means the plant does not have to raise temperature only to

59
00:02:56,831 --> 00:03:00,116
get a useful rate, which would lower the ammonia yield.

60
00:03:00,232 --> 00:03:01,440
Compare the options.

61
00:03:01,486 --> 00:03:05,120
Three hundred degrees Celsius at two hundred atmospheres gives a

62
00:03:05,236 --> 00:03:08,336
high yield but is too slow for efficient production.

63
00:03:08,452 --> 00:03:12,284
Six hundred and fifty degrees Celsius at two hundred atmospheres

64
00:03:12,353 --> 00:03:16,022
is fast, but the ammonia equilibrium yield is reduced.

65
00:03:16,150 --> 00:03:19,981
Four hundred and fifty degrees Celsius, two hundred atmospheres

66
00:03:20,039 --> 00:03:23,951
and iron gives a useful yield and a useful rate.

67
00:03:24,160 --> 00:03:28,108
Plant X is offered three hundred and fifty, four hundred

68
00:03:28,142 --> 00:03:32,067
and fifty or six hundred degrees Celsius, and one hundred,

69
00:03:32,090 --> 00:03:36,142
two hundred or four hundred atmospheres, with iron available.

70
00:03:36,478 --> 00:03:40,147
Three hundred and fifty degrees Celsius has a higher yield

71
00:03:40,356 --> 00:03:41,401
but is slower.

72
00:03:41,854 --> 00:03:45,534
Six hundred degrees Celsius has a higher rate but a

73
00:03:45,615 --> 00:03:46,451
lower yield.

74
00:03:46,730 --> 00:03:50,805
Four hundred atmospheres gives more yield but more compression

75
00:03:50,886 --> 00:03:51,397
cost.

76
00:03:51,734 --> 00:03:55,670
One hundred atmospheres costs less but gives a lower yield

77
00:03:55,821 --> 00:03:57,632
than two hundred atmospheres.

78
00:03:58,027 --> 00:04:01,823
The indicative industrial choice is about four hundred and fifty

79
00:04:01,881 --> 00:04:05,840
degrees Celsius and about two hundred atmospheres with iron.

80
00:04:06,204 --> 00:04:07,760
Correct the misconceptions.

81
00:04:07,934 --> 00:04:11,904
The lowest possible temperature is not chosen, because the rate

82
00:04:11,951 --> 00:04:13,042
would be too slow.

83
00:04:13,379 --> 00:04:17,501
The highest possible pressure is not chosen, because of energy,

84
00:04:17,559 --> 00:04:18,964
equipment and cost.

85
00:04:19,207 --> 00:04:22,075
Iron does not increase equilibrium yield.

86
00:04:22,284 --> 00:04:26,139
Higher temperature does not always improve production, because

87
00:04:26,185 --> 00:04:30,086
for this reaction it decreases the ammonia equilibrium

88
00:04:30,179 --> 00:04:30,678
yield.

89
00:04:31,293 --> 00:04:33,372
Examination-style question.

90
00:04:33,708 --> 00:04:34,614
Part a.

91
00:04:35,067 --> 00:04:38,561
State typical temperature, pressure and catalyst.

92
00:04:38,956 --> 00:04:39,862
Three marks.

93
00:04:40,315 --> 00:04:44,285
About four hundred and fifty degrees Celsius, about two hundred

94
00:04:44,401 --> 00:04:46,340
atmospheres, and iron.

95
00:04:47,048 --> 00:04:47,757
Part b.

96
00:04:48,000 --> 00:04:51,959
Why is a lower temperature not used, even though yield

97
00:04:52,006 --> 00:04:52,900
would be higher?

98
00:04:53,515 --> 00:04:54,351
Three marks.

99
00:04:54,874 --> 00:04:58,717
Lower temperature favours the exothermic forward reaction,

100
00:04:58,833 --> 00:05:02,803
so yield is higher, but the rate is lower, so production

101
00:05:02,873 --> 00:05:06,228
is too slow or less economically efficient.

102
00:05:06,844 --> 00:05:07,645
Part c.

103
00:05:08,098 --> 00:05:10,594
Why does higher pressure increase yield?

104
00:05:11,418 --> 00:05:12,161
Two marks.

105
00:05:12,278 --> 00:05:16,074
There are two moles of gas against four, so the equilibrium shifts

106
00:05:16,132 --> 00:05:17,351
toward ammonia.

107
00:05:17,630 --> 00:05:18,361
Part d.

108
00:05:18,535 --> 00:05:21,345
Why is industrial pressure a compromise?

109
00:05:21,624 --> 00:05:22,506
Three marks.

110
00:05:22,564 --> 00:05:24,874
Higher pressure increases yield.

111
00:05:25,118 --> 00:05:28,799
Very high pressure increases compression, equipment and

112
00:05:28,880 --> 00:05:29,391
cost.

113
00:05:29,634 --> 00:05:33,477
Industry balances yield against economic and engineering

114
00:05:33,559 --> 00:05:34,116
cost.

115
00:05:34,453 --> 00:05:35,323
Part e.

116
00:05:35,660 --> 00:05:38,121
What does the iron catalyst do?

117
00:05:38,330 --> 00:05:39,155
Two marks.

118
00:05:39,491 --> 00:05:43,195
It provides a lower activation-energy pathway,

119
00:05:43,404 --> 00:05:47,571
so rate increases and equilibrium is reached faster,

120
00:05:47,653 --> 00:05:50,288
without changing the equilibrium position.

121
00:05:50,683 --> 00:05:53,109
Before we finish, complete the model.

122
00:05:53,562 --> 00:05:57,219
Haber ammonia is made at about four hundred and fifty

123
00:05:57,277 --> 00:06:01,155
degrees Celsius and about two hundred atmospheres

124
00:06:01,248 --> 00:06:02,154
with iron.

125
00:06:02,606 --> 00:06:06,496
Temperature and pressure are compromises between yield, rate

126
00:06:06,542 --> 00:06:07,332
and cost.

127
00:06:07,483 --> 00:06:11,268
The catalyst speeds both directions without changing

128
00:06:11,349 --> 00:06:14,611
the equilibrium yield at fixed conditions.

